review.notes:
No misformalization found. Details of the verification:
(1) Definitions. `S' h k = Σ_{j∈[1,k)} (-1)^(j+1+⌊hj/k⌋₊)` and `S k = Σ_{h∈[1,k)} S' h k` match the paper's (1.1) and (1.2). In Lean I proved that `⌊(h*j:ℚ)/k⌋₊ = h*j/k` (`Nat.floor_div_eq_div`), so `S'` is exactly the natural-division form. A C program using the same arithmetic agrees with Lean's `#eval S` for k ≤ 30 and at k = 5, 7, 113, 179, 233, 239, 241. It reproduces every number in the paper I checked:
- exactly 17 primes < 10000 with S(k) < 2k, the last being 233;
- exactly 87 primes < 10000 with S(k) < 3k, the last being 3119;
- S(p) > p for all primes 7 ≤ p ≤ 10000, and S(p) > 0 for all primes ≤ 10000;
- S(945)=-296, S(2079)=-1390, S(3465)=-7800, S(5005)=-1332, S(8855)=-7950, S(9975)=-22450, S(10395)=-40726, S(17017)=-2364 and S(19019)=-20578;
- v3's pairs (32603,126466), (33149,126068) and (49139,189522).
So the thresholds in the file (k>5, k>233, k>3119, all strict) are the right ones.
(2) The `S_fst_10` note is right. Table 1 is wrong in both v1 and v3 from k=9 on: the true values are S(9..19) = 8, 17, 14, 21, 20, 15, 18, 39, 24, 21, 38, which are the table's entries for k=10..20. The table's values also violate the paper's own parity proposition (S(k) and k have opposite parity).
(3) Statuses. All `research open` tags are consistent with the sources. v3 (Jan 2026) says the conjectures are open, and OpenAlex records no citing works for the arXiv or journal version. I did not read the Springer journal text itself; I assume it matches v2/v3.
(4) Changes in later versions. v3 dropped v1's Conjecture 4.1; it follows from v3's Conjecture 3.1 plus the finite check for 7 ≤ p ≤ 233. v3 added an upper bound, S(k) < C2·k^{1+ε} for all C2>0 and ε>0 eventually for primes k, which is not formalized here. v3 also refutes an informal v1 remark, not formalized in the file, that negative S(k) occur only at multiples of 3 or 5: S(17017) = -2364. The four `conjecture_4_4_def_*` test implications are proven, correctly stated and axiom-clean.
